.TH "TRUSTEDHTML.TOJSON" "3JS" "October 30, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
TrustedHTML.toJSON \- TrustedHTML: toJSON() method
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]Note:\f[R] This feature is available in Web Workers.
.PP
The \f[B]\f[CB]toJSON()\f[B]\f[R] method of the \f[CR]TrustedHTML\f[R]
interface returns a string representing the \f[CR]TrustedHTML\f[R]
object.
This is the same string that \f[CR]TrustedHTML.toString()\f[R] returns.
.PP
The method is automatically called by \f[CR]JSON.stringify()\f[R] when a
\f[CR]TrustedHTML\f[R] object is encountered during JSON serialization.
The serialized value can then be converted back using the
\f[CR]TrustedTypePolicy.createHTML()\f[R] method within the reviver of
\f[CR]JSON.parse()\f[R], which runs the policy\(cqs transformation on it
again.
.SH SYNTAX
.IP
.EX
toJSON()
.EE
.SS Parameters
None.
.SS Return value
A string.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Calling toJSON() directly
This example creates a \f[CR]TrustedHTML\f[R] object.
Calling \f[CR]toJSON()\f[R] directly returns its stored string value.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] escapeHTMLPolicy = trustedTypes.createPolicy(\(dqmyEscapePolicy\(dq, {
  createHTML: (string) \f[B]=>\f[R] string.replace(/</g, \(dq&lt;\(dq),
});

\f[B]const\f[R] escaped = escapeHTMLPolicy.createHTML(\(dq<img src=x onerror=alert(1)>\(dq);

\f[B]const\f[R] json = escaped.toJSON();
console.log(json); \f[I]// Same value as escaped.toString()\f[R]
console.log(\f[B]typeof\f[R] json); \f[I]// \(dqstring\(dq\f[R]
.EE
.SS Serializing to a JSON string
The \f[CR]TrustedHTML\f[R] object from the previous example is
serialized using \f[CR]JSON.stringify()\f[R], which calls the
\f[CR]toJSON()\f[R] method automatically.
.IP
.EX
console.log(JSON.stringify(escaped));
.EE
.PP
This would log the following JSON string, including the surrounding
double quotes, which are part of the string\(cqs content:
.IP
.EX
\(dq&lt;img src=x onerror=alert(1)>\(dq
.EE
